<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Almoravid Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Almoravids, Almoravid dynasty, Almoravid, al-Murabitun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Almoravid Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> Muslim empire that arose from a religious reform movement among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> in the eleventh century. Under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> it conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, and intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> after 1086. It promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and controlled trans-Saharan trade, influencing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, before it was overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in 1147.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire.jpg" alt="Almoravid Empire" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Expansi%C3%B3nAlmor%C3%A1vide.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
